"""
The network checks of a web application:
the address of the client behind proxies that are trusted, the host name,
and the address that an outbound request may go to.

clientAddress starts from the peer, the address the web server names in
REMOTE_ADDR, and believes a forwarding header only when the peer is itself
in the list of trusted proxies.  With an empty list the peer is the client
and every header is ignored: a client that forges X-Forwarded-For changes
nothing.  The list must name the direct hop only, never a whole chain such
as the ranges of a CDN, and it is worth anything only while that hop cannot
be reached from the internet; behind Hiawatha over a Unix socket REMOTE_ADDR
is what Hiawatha resolved already, and the list stays empty.

The result is an address in the canonical text of Standard.Ip, or, when
the peer is no address, the peer as it came.
"""
import Standard.String (
    String
);

module Wolly.Net is
    """
    The most bytes of a host name: 253.
    """
    function mostHostnameBytes(): Index
        ensures result = 253;

    """
    Whether text is a host name: labels of letters, digits and hyphens, none
    empty, none longer than 63 bytes, none with a hyphen first or last,
    separated by dots, at most 253 bytes in all.  No trailing dot (it would
    have to be cut by everyone who puts the name in a URL, and two
    spellings of one host invite a cache keyed twice), no star, no
    underscore, no address (an address is no name; see isPublicAddress),
    no Unicode (punycode first).
    No contract: a test of outside input; false is its answer for a bad
    one.
    """
    generic [T: Region]
    function validHostname(text: Span[Nat8, T]): Bool;

    """
    Whether text is an address that a request of the server may go to: an
    IPv4 or IPv6 address that Standard.Ip.isPrivateOrReserved does not
    name.  False for text that is no address, for a name (resolving it is
    for the caller, and every address it resolves to must pass), and for the
    empty text.
    No contract: a test of outside input; false is its answer for a bad
    one.
    """
    generic [T: Region]
    function isPublicAddress(text: Span[Nat8, T]): Bool;

    """
    Whether trusted is a list of trusted proxies: addresses or prefixes in
    the form address/bits, separated by commas, with spaces around an
    entry allowed.  An empty list is a list (it trusts nobody).  False for
    an entry that is no address, a number of bits that is no number or too
    large, an empty entry, and a prefix of 0 bits, which would trust every
    peer.
    No contract: a test of outside input; false is its answer for a bad
    one.
    """
    generic [T: Region]
    function validTrustedProxies(trusted: Span[Nat8, T]): Bool;

    """
    The address of the client.  peer is REMOTE_ADDR, forwarded is the value
    of X-Forwarded-For (may be empty), trusted a list as validTrustedProxies
    says.  When the list is empty or invalid, or the peer is not in it, or
    is no address: the peer.  Otherwise the forwarded addresses are walked
    from the right, the last the proxy appended first, and the first one
    that is not trusted is the client; a part that is no address ends the
    walk, and a chain that is empty, wholly trusted or ends so gives the
    peer.
    No contract: the length of the result depends on the text, and the
    peer it may give back may be empty.
    """
    generic [P: Region, F: Region, T: Region]
    function clientAddress(peer: Span[Nat8, P], forwarded: Span[Nat8, F], trusted: Span[Nat8, T]): String;
end module.
